{"name":"Hot Pepper Green","hex":"#598039","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Hote."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Relating to heat and conditions which produce it.\n Having or giving off a high temperature.","Having or giving off a high temperature."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hot"},{"term":"pepper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the family Piperaceae.","A spice prepared from the fermented, dried, unripe berries of this plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add pepper to.","To strike with something made up of small particles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pepper"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chili pepper","blocks":[{"text":"Chili peppers, also spelled chile or chilli, are varieties of berry-fruit plants from the genus Capsicum, which are members of the nightshade family Solanaceae, cultivated for their pungency. They are used as a spice to add pungency in many cuisines. Capsaicin and the related capsaicinoids give chili peppers their intensity when ingested or applied topically. Chili peppers exhibit a range of heat and flavors. This diversity is the reason behind the availability of different types of chili powder, each offering its own taste and heat level."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Madame_Jeanette_and_other_chillies.jpg/330px-Madame_Jeanette_and_other_chillies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chili_pepper","wikidata":"Q165199"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Leiden_University_Library_-_Seikei_Zusetsu_vol._25%2C_page_019_-_%E8%95%83%E6%A4%92_-_Capsicum_annuum_L.%2C_1804_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Leiden_University_Library_-_Seikei_Zusetsu_vol._25%2C_page_019_-_%E8%95%83%E6%A4%92_-_Capsicum_annuum_L.%2C_1804_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Leiden_University_Library_-_Seikei_Zusetsu_vol._25%2C_page_019_-_%E8%95%83%E6%A4%92_-_Capsicum_annuum_L.%2C_1804_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Leiden_University_Library_-_Seikei_Zusetsu_vol._25%2C_page_019_-_%E8%95%83%E6%A4%92_-_Capsicum_annuum_L.%2C_